 ## Overview
-Bounding Volume Optimization minimizes the computational cost of voxel-based rendering by restricting evaluation to the spatial extents of active geometry, thereby eliminating false positives and skipping expensive Signed Distance Field (SDF) evaluations for objects that do not contribute to the minimal distance field [S1, unofficial][S2].
+Bounding Volume Optimization minimizes the computational cost of voxel-based rendering by restricting evaluation to the spatial extents of active geometry, thereby eliminating false positives and skipping expensive Signed Distance Field (SDF) evaluations for objects that do not contribute to the minimal distance field [S1, unofficial][S2]. Unlike [3D Voxel Grid Smoothing](833-3d-voxel-grid-smoothing.md) which modifies field values or [Binary Voxel RLE Encoding](1071-binary-voxel-rle-encoding.md) which compresses storage, this method focuses on spatial culling and dynamic array resizing to skip evaluation entirely [S1, unofficial][S2]. It builds on [Voxel Grid Optimization](4131-voxel-grid-optimization.md) by applying bounding volume tests before ray traversal.
 
 ## How it works
+**Inputs and Outputs:**
+*   **Inputs:** Ray origin $O$, ray direction $D$, list of scene primitives with associated SDF evaluators and axis-aligned bounding boxes (AABBs), target voxel size.
+*   **Outputs:** Adjusted voxel array dimensions $(W, H, D)$, list of active primitives intersected by the ray for SDF evaluation.
+
 **Data Structures:**
 *   **Bounding Volume (BV):** An axis-aligned bounding box (AABB) defined by minimum and maximum coordinates $(x_{min}, y_{min}, z_{min})$ and $(x_{max}, y_{max}, z_{max})$ [G].
